    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Normal faults in the eastern margin of the Gulf of Suez, Egypt, intersect a 50m thick shale unit that has been smeared along the fault planes. A regional and a local fault examples were examined to determine the relationship between stratigraphic fault offset and thickness of shale smear along the fault. The thickness of the shale in the fault zone, and the stratigraphic offset cross the fault were determined for each fault example. Results show that the shale smear thickness decreases with fault displacement following a log-normal relationship. The 50 meters of shale unit was thinned down to zero thickness as the fault displacement reaches 200 - 300 m, which indicates a shale smear ratio (fault throw/shale source layer thickness) of 4-6. This ratio is identical for two faults with different displacement magnitudes, and hence can be used for subsurface fault sealing evaluation.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
